{"id":"7e9d0d95-a007-495e-bad2-5006a21f2675","object":"chat.completion","created":1784983810,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    retained = set()\\n    # all non-removable nodes must be retained\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            retained.add(n['id'])\\n    # ensure ancestors of retained nodes are also retained\\n    def add_ancestors(retained_set, nmap):\\n        changed = True\\n        while changed:\\n            changed = False\\n            for nid in list(retained_set):\\n                parent_id = nmap[nid]['parent']\\n                if parent_id is not None and parent_id not in retained_set:\\n                    retained_set.add(parent_id)\\n                    changed = True\\n    # After initial non-removable, ensure ancestors (non-removable parent might be removable?)\\n    add_ancestors(retained, node_map)\\n    # For each required symbol, ensure it is covered\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        candidates = [n for n in nodes if n['symbol'] == sym]\\n        # check if any candidate is already retained (and non-removable, but we already have all non-removable)\\n        if any(n['id'] in retained for n in candidates):\\n            continue\\n        # need to retain at least one candidate and its ancestors\\n        # choose the one with smallest depth to minimize added nodes\\n        rem_candidates = [n for n in candidates if n['id'] not in retained]\\n        if not rem_candidates:\\n            continue\\n        chosen = min(rem_candidates, key=lambda n: n['depth'])\\n        # add chosen and ancestors\\n        cur = chosen\\n        while cur['parent'] is not None and cur['id'] not in retained:\\n            retained.add(cur['id'])\\n            cur = node_map[cur['parent']]\\n        # add the root if not already\\n        if cur['id'] not in retained:\\n            retained.add(cur['id'])\\n        # now ensure all ancestors of newly added nodes are retained\\n        add_ancestors(retained, node_map)\\n    # after all, build result lists\\n    removed_ids = [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in retained]\\n    retained_ids = [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] in retained]\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed_ids, 'retained_node_ids': retained_ids}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a Python function solve(case) that takes a payload (the inner object from the fixture rows) and returns a JSON object with \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\" arrays of strings. The task is about \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" with a bounded objective: reduce a parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement while preserving an interestingness oracle. The fixture provides nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. The required_symbols list indicates which symbols must be preserved. We need to decide which nodes to remove and which to retain. The root node is not removable.\n\nThe goal: reduce the tree by removing as many removable nodes as possible while still satisfying the required_symbols constraint? The \"bounded objective\" might be to maximize utility loss? Actually typical pruning: keep required symbols, and maybe maximize retained utility or minimize removal of high-utility nodes? But the output is just two lists of IDs. The fixture is simple: a tree with root and children. The required symbols: \"root\" and \"s1\". So we must keep at least one node with symbol \"root\" (the root itself) and at least one node with symbol \"s1\". Among the nodes, we have removable flags. We can prune removable nodes that are not needed for the required symbols. But we must also consider that removing a node might cascade: if a node is removed, its children are presumably also removed? The problem says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement\" - maybe we can only remove nodes if they are superfluous, but children of removed nodes are also removed. We need to decide which removable nodes to keep or remove.\n\nGiven the fixture: there are 7 nodes. root (depth0, not removable), n1 (depth1, not removable, symbol s1), n2 (depth1, removable, symbol s2), n3 (depth1, not removable, symbol s0), n4 (depth2, parent n2, removable, symbol s1), n5 (depth2, parent n3, not removable, symbol s2), n6 (depth2, parent n1, removable, symbol s0). Required symbols: root, s1.\n\nWe need to output removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. We must decide which nodes to remove. Intuitively, we can remove n2 (removable, symbol s2) because its presence is not required, but its child n4 has symbol s1 which is required. If we remove n2, we lose n4, so we need to consider alternative paths to get s1. The root has child n1 which is s1 and not removable, so we already have s1 from n1. So we can safely remove n2 and n4, and also n6 (removable) because s0 is not required. n5 is not removable, so must retain. So removed: n2, n4, n6; retained: root, n1, n3, n5. That seems plausible. For all three fixtures, the pattern is similar: required symbols root and s1, removable nodes n2, n4, n6, non-removable: root, n1, n3, n5. So the answer would be consistent.\n\nBut we need to write a general algorithm that works for any payload. The case payload contains nodes list and required_symbols list. We need to find a set of nodes to remove such that the remaining nodes form a valid subtree (connected?) and include at least one node for each required symbol. Also, the nodes have a parent pointer, so it's a tree. We need to decide which removable nodes to keep or remove. Possibly we should keep the minimum number of nodes to satisfy required symbols, while respecting that non-removable nodes must be kept. Also, we might want to maximize utility? The output only needs to list removed and retained IDs. The task doesn't clarify