etained, but their children may be removable. However, if a non-removable node is retained, its removable children can be removed if they are not needed. But if a non-removable node is required to be retained, we must retain it. In the fixture, n3 is non-removable but its symbol s0 is not required. Yet we must retain n3. Its child n5 is non-removable, so also retained.\n\nSo algorithm:\n1. Build node map from nodes list.\n2. Identify root node (parent null). Must be retained.\n3. Mark all non-removable nodes as \"must_retain\".\n4. For each required symbol, we need to ensure that at least one node with that symbol is retained. We can choose among nodes with that symbol (including removable ones). To minimize removals, we could keep the easiest paths. However, we need to ensure connectivity: if we retain a removable node, its ancestors up to root must also be retained (some may be removable, some not). We should prefer retaining nodes that are already required (non-removable) if possible.\n   - For each required symbol, find all nodes with that symbol. Among those, we want to see if any is non-removable (must be retained anyway). If so, symbol satisfied automatically.\n   - If no non-removable node has that symbol, we must retain at least one removable node with that symbol, and to connect it, we may need to retain its ancestor chain (which could include removable nodes). So we should choose the one with minimal \"cost\" (e.g., fewest additional removable nodes to retain, or based on utility?). Since we don't have a clear objective, we can aim to retain the one that requires retaining the fewest additional removable nodes. Or we might just pick any and retain its entire path.\n   - Then, after deciding which nodes to retain for required symbols, we mark all nodes on their paths to root as retained (if they are removable, we must retain them too). Non-removable nodes are already retained.\n   - Then, all remaining nodes (not marked retained) can be removed, provided they are removable. But also, any node that is not removable and not yet retained must be retained (shouldn't happen because all non-removable are already included? Actually if a non-removable node is not on any required path, we still must retain it. So we should initially mark all non-removable nodes as retained.\n5. Then, retained set = all nodes marked retained. Removed set = all other nodes (which are removable). However, we also need to check if any removable nodes that are not retained but their children are retained? That can't happen because for a child to be retained, its parent must be retained (we ensured by ancestral retention). So that's consistent.\n6. Output lists of IDs.\n\nBut we need to be careful: The required_symbols might include symbols that have no nodes? That is unlikely.\n\nAlso, note that root is non-removable and its symbol is \"root\". So root is always retained.\n\nGiven the fixture, all non-removable nodes: root, n1 (s1), n3 (s0), n5 (s2). They are all retained. Removable nodes: n2, n4, n6. Among these, we check required symbol s1: we already have n1 non-removable with s1, so no need to retain n4. So all removable nodes are removed. That matches.\n\nFor a more complex case, we may need to make choices. Since the task doesn't define an optimization metric, we might just implement a simple strategy: retain all non-removable, then for each required symbol, if no non-removable node with that symbol exists, select a removable node with that symbol that is \"closest\" to root (to minimize retained removable ancestors) and retain its entire ancestor chain. That should produce a valid solution. The output just requires the lists, not an optimal one.\n\nWe need to implement solve(case) that returns dict with removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nNow, we need to encode the Python source as a JSON string under \"implementation\". Use standard library only.\n\nLet's write the algorithm in Python. We'll parse the case payload.\n\nPseudo:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']  # list\n    # Build dict\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    # Determine root: parent is None\n    root_id = None\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n['parent'] is None:\n            root_id = n['id']\n            break\n    # Mark retained nodes\n    retained = set()\n    # All non-removable nodes must be retained\n    for n in nodes:\n        if not n['removable']:\n            retained.add(n['id'])\n    # For each required symbol, ensure it is present in retained\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        # Find nodes with this symbol\n        candidates = [n for n in nodes if n['symbol'] == sym]\n        # Check if any non-removable candidate is already in retained (it should be)\n        has_nonremovable = any(n['id'] in retained and not n['removable'] for n in candidates)\n        if has_nonremovable:\n            continue\n        # Need to retain at least one candidate. Choose the one with smallest depth? Or any.\n        # Since we need to retain ancestors, we want to minimize additional nodes.\n        # Find candidates that are not already retained (they are removable)\n        rem_candidates = [n for n in candidates if n['id'] not in retained]\n        if not rem_candidates:\n            # Should not happen if required_symbol is present but all such nodes are non-removable? handled.\n            continue\n        # Choose one with smallest depth (closest to root) to minimize ancestors\n        chosen = min(rem_candidates, key=lambda n: n['depth'])\n        # Retain chosen and all its ancestors up to root\n        # Also note: ancestors might already be retained\n        current = chosen\n        while current is not None:\n            if current['id'] not in retained:\n                retained.add(current['id'])\n            if current['parent'] is None:\n                break\n            current = node_map[current['parent']]\n    # After ensuring required symbols, we have retained set.\n    # All nodes not in retained are removable (by construction, non-removable are retained)\n    removed_ids = [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in retained]\n    retained_ids = [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] in retained]\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids}\n\nWe should also ensure that for nodes that are retained, all ancestors are retained. Our while loop ensures that for the chosen candidate, but what if a non-removable node's parent is removable and not retained? That shouldn't happen because non-removable node must have a parent that is also retained? Actually, if a non-removable node's parent is removable, the parent might be removed but the child is non-removable, which would be invalid because the child can't exist without parent. The input might guarantee that removable nodes are only leaves? Not necessarily. But we should ensure that for any retained node, its parent is also retained. In our algorithm, we mark all non-removable nodes as retained. If a non-removable node has a removable parent, we did not automatically retain that parent. That could break the tree structure. For example, if a non-removable node n5 has parent n3 which is non-removable, fine. But if n4 (removable) had a non-removable child? Not in example. However, to be safe, we should propagate upwards: after marking all non-removable nodes, we should ensure that for each retained node, its ancestors are also retained. So we should do a second pass: for each retained node, walk up to root and ensure all nodes on the path are added to retained. That handles both non-removable and required-symbol additions.\n\nLet's incorporate: after initial marking of non-removable, do for each node in a copy of retained (or iterate over all nodes), for each retained node, ensure ancestors are retained.\n\nSimplify: After we have a set of initially retained (non-removable + required symbol chains), we can do a fixpoint: while there is a retained node whose parent is not retained, add parent to retained. Since parent pointer goes up, this is straightforward.\n\nTo avoid infinite loops, we can just iterate: for each retained node, add its ancestors recursively.\n\nImplementation:\n\ndef ensure_ancestors(retained, node_map):\n    added = True\n    while added:\n        added = False\n        for node_id in list(retained):\n            parent_id = node_map[node_id]['parent']\n            if parent_id is not None and parent_id not in retained:\n                retained.add(parent_id)\n                added = True\n\nWe'll call this after building initial retained set.\n\nAlso, note: for nodes that are removable but we retain them (e.g., chosen candidate for required symbol), we need to ensure their ancestors are retained, which we already do.\n\nNow, required symbols: we need to ensure at least one node per symbol is retained after everything. Our loop might add more nodes. So final check: after ensuring ancestors, we might have more nodes retained, but the required symbol condition might already be satisfied by non-removable nodes. If not, we add a chain. So algorithm should work.\n\nEdge case: what if there is a required symbol that is present only on a removable node that is a descendant of another removable node that we chose to remove? Then we need to retain that chain. Our selection picks one candidate and retains its ancestors, so it's fine.\n\nNow, we need to write the code with only standard library and not mutate case. We'll create a new dict.\n\nI'll write the function in the implementation string.\n\nNow, output format: JSON object with \"implementation\" key containing a JSON-escaped string of the Python source code.\n\nI'll produce that.\n\nNote: The function signature must be exactly solve(case). No additional imports needed besides standard.\n\nLet's produce the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1928,"completion_token